{% doc %}
  How many slides are visible at one breakpoint. Returns a bare number.

  ## Why this is its own snippet

  Two places need this answer and they must never disagree:

    `carousel-options`  puts it in `data-options` for Swiper, which uses it
                        *after* the module loads.
    `carousel-vars`     puts it in a CSS custom property, which sizes the slides
                        *before* the module loads.

  If the two ever drift, the slides resize the moment Swiper mounts — which is
  the visible jump this snippet exists to prevent. So the arithmetic lives here
  once and both callers render it rather than restating it.

  ## Peek

  A partial slide at the edge is not a separate layout, it is a fractional
  `slidesPerView`. "Two columns with a 30% peek" is `2.3`: two whole slides plus
  three tenths of the next one. Expressing it that way means Swiper's snapping,
  the progress bar, the loop guard and the pre-init CSS all keep working with no
  special cases — a peek is just a number with a decimal in it.

  ## No effect handling

  This used to force `columns` to 1 when the transition was `fade`, because a
  cross-fade of a stack is only coherent with one slide showing. That is what
  silently collapsed a three-across testimonials carousel to one the moment fade
  was selected. The setting is gone — every carousel slides — so the branch is
  gone with it.

  Peek is split at 750px, not per breakpoint. A phone almost always wants one and
  a wide multi-column grid almost never does; a third control for tablets would
  be a setting merchants have to reason about for a difference nobody sees.

  ## Parameters

    settings (object, required)  Section or block settings
    viewport (string, required)  mobile | tablet | desktop
    columns_mobile / columns_tablet / columns_desktop (number)  Overrides
    peek_mobile  (number)   Overrides `settings.carousel_peek_mobile`
    peek_desktop (number)   Overrides `settings.carousel_peek_desktop`

  @example
    {%- capture spv -%}{% render 'carousel-slides', settings: section.settings, viewport: 'mobile' %}{%- endcapture -%}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  case viewport
    when 'desktop'
      assign columns = columns_desktop | default: settings.columns_desktop | default: 4
      assign peek = peek_desktop
      if peek == nil
        assign peek = settings.carousel_peek_desktop
      endif
    when 'tablet'
      assign columns = columns_tablet | default: settings.columns_tablet | default: 2
      assign peek = peek_desktop
      if peek == nil
        assign peek = settings.carousel_peek_desktop
      endif
    else
      assign columns = columns_mobile | default: settings.columns_mobile | default: 1
      assign peek = peek_mobile
      if peek == nil
        assign peek = settings.carousel_peek_mobile
      endif
  endcase

  assign columns = columns | plus: 0
  if columns < 1
    assign columns = 1
  endif

  assign peek = peek | default: 0 | plus: 0

  # Capped below one whole slide. At 100 the "partial" slide is a whole extra
  # column, which is the columns setting with extra steps, and above that the
  # arithmetic stops matching what the merchant sees.
  if peek < 0
    assign peek = 0
  endif
  if peek > 90
    assign peek = 90
  endif

  # `100.0` rather than `100`: integer division would floor every peek to zero.
  assign slides = columns
  if peek > 0
    assign fraction = peek | divided_by: 100.0
    assign slides = columns | plus: fraction
  endif
-%}
{{- slides -}}